b:\n\n(3p -4q) \u00d7 (p +3q) = 3p\u00d7p + 9p\u00d7q -4q\u00d7p -12q\u00d7q\n\nYes, p\u00d7p and q\u00d7q are zero. Then 9p\u00d7q -4q\u00d7p. Since q\u00d7p = -p\u00d7q, so -4q\u00d7p = 4p\u00d7q. Therefore, total is 9p\u00d7q +4p\u00d7q =13p\u00d7q. So that's correct. Then the magnitude is 13 times |p\u00d7q|. |p\u00d7q| is |p||q|sin\u03b8. The angle between p and q is \u03c0/4, so sin(\u03c0/4)=\u221a2/2. So 2*3*(\u221a2/2)= 3\u221a2. Multiply by 13 gives 39\u221a2. So the area is 39\u221a2. That seems right.\n\nWait, let me verify once more. Maybe I should compute |a \u00d7 b| step by step again. Alternatively, maybe use another method to confirm the result. For example, compute the area using the formula |a||b|sin\u03c6, where \u03c6 is the angle between a and b. But then I would need to find the magnitude of a and b and the angle between them, which might involve more steps, but could be a good cross-check.\n\nFirst, let's compute |a| and |b|.\n\nCompute |a|: a =3p -4q. The magnitude squared is (3p -4q)\u00b7(3p -4q) = 9|p|\u00b2 + 16|q|\u00b2 -24p\u00b7q\n\nSimilarly, |b|: b = p +3q. The magnitude squared is |p|\u00b2 +9|q|\u00b2 +6p\u00b7q\n\nFirst compute p\u00b7q. Since p\u00b7q = |p||q|cos\u03b8 = 2*3*cos(\u03c0/4) = 6*(\u221a2/2) = 3\u221a2.\n\nSo p\u00b7q = 3\u221a2.\n\nThen |a|\u00b2 = 9*(2\u00b2) +16*(3\u00b2) -24*(3\u221a2) = 9*4 +16*9 -24*3\u221a2 = 36 +144 -72\u221a2 = 180 -72\u221a2.\n\nTherefore, |a| = sqrt(180 -72\u221a2). Hmm, that's a bit messy. Similarly, |b|\u00b2 = 2\u00b2 +9*(3\u00b2) +6*(3\u221a2) = 4 +81 +18\u221a2 = 85 +18\u221a2. So |b| = sqrt(85 +18\u221a2).\n\nNow, the area is |a||b|sin\u03c6, where \u03c6 is the angle between a and b. But this seems complicated because calculating sin\u03c6 would require knowing cos\u03c6 first, which is (a\u00b7b)/(|a||b|). Then sin\u03c6 = sqrt(1 - cos\u00b2\u03c6). But this might get too involved. Let's see:\n\nAlternatively, since we already calculated |a \u00d7 b| as 39\u221a2, which is suppos