ation. However, due to the function's even nature and the analysis showing that for each p \u2208 (0,2], there are two real solutions, the formula should hold.\n\nTherefore, the final answer is:\n\nFor each real number p such that 0 < p \u22642, the solutions for x are:\n\nx= \u00b1\u221a[1 - ((p\u00b3 - 2)/(3p))\u00b3]\n\nFor p outside this interval, there are no real solutions.\n\n**Final Answer**\nThe solutions are \\(\\boxed{\\pm \\sqrt{1 - \\left( \\frac{p^3 - 2}{3p} \\right)^3}}\\) for \\(0 < p \\leq 2\\).", "deepseek_solution": "Given the equation involving the sum of cube roots:\n\n\\[\n\\sqrt[3]{1 - x} + \\sqrt[3]{1 + x} = p\n\\]\n\nwe aim to find all values of \\( x \\) such that the equation holds for an arbitrary real number \\( p \\).\n\n1. **Introduce variables for simplification**:\n   Let \\( a = \\sqrt[3]{1 - x} \\) and \\( b = \\sqrt[3]{1 + x} \\). Then, the equation becomes:\n   \\[\n   a + b = p\n   \\]\n   Cubing both sides:\n   \\[\n   (a + b)^3 = p^3\n   \\]\n   Expanding the left-hand side:\n   \\[\n   a^3 + b^3 + 3ab(a + b) = p^3\n   \\]\n\n2. **Substitute known values**:\n   Since \\( a^3 = 1 - x \\) and \\( b^3 = 1 + x \\), we have:\n   \\[\n   (1 - x) + (1 + x) + 3ab(a + b) = p^3\n   \\]\n   Simplifying:\n   \\[\n   2 + 3abp = p^3\n   \\]\n   Solving for \\( ab \\):\n   \\[\n   ab = \\frac{p^3 - 2}{3p}\n   \\]\n\n3. **Relate \\( ab \\) to \\( x \\)**:\n   The product \\( ab \\) can be expressed as:\n   \\[\n   ab = \\sqrt[3]{(1 - x)(1 + x)} = \\sqrt[3]{1 - x^2}\n   \\]\n   Cubing both sides:\n   \\[\n   \\sqrt[3]{1 - x^2} = \\frac{p^3 - 2}{3p}\n   \\]\n   Therefore:\n   \\[\n   1 - x^2 = \\left( \\frac{p^3 - 2}{3p} \\right)^3\n   \\]\n\n4. **Solve for \\( x \\)**:\n   Rearranging to solve for \\( x^2 \\):\n   \\[\n   x^2 = 1 - \\left( \\frac{p^3 - 2}{3p} \\right)^3\n   \\]\n   Taking the square root:\n   \\[\n   x = \\pm \\sqrt{1 - \\left( \\frac{p^3 - 2}{3p} \\right)^3}\n   \\]\n\n5. **Determine valid \\( p \\) values**:\n   The expression under the square root must be non-negative:\n   \\[\n   1 - \\left( \\frac{p^3 - 2}{3p} \\right)^3 \\geq 0\n   \\]\n   Analyzing the function \\( f(x) = \\sqrt[3]{1 - x} + \\sqrt[3]{1 + x} \\), it is found that the range of \\( f(x) \\) is \\( (0, 2] \\). Therefore, \\( p \\) must be in this interval.\n\n**Final Answer**:\nThe solutions for \\( x \\) are given by:\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\pm \\sqrt{1 - \\left( \\frac{p^3 - 2}{3p} \\right)^3}}\n\\]\nfor \\( 0 < p \\leq 2 \\). For \\( p \\) outside this interval, there are no real solutions.", "ground_truth_solution": "\n1. First, denote \\( u^3 = 1 - x \\) and \\( v^3 = 1 + x \\).\n2. Given the equation \\( u + v = p \\), we begin by substituting \\( u \\) and \\( v \\) into this equation.\n\n3. We will use the identity \\( u^3 + v^3 = (u + v)(u^2 - uv + v^2) \\).\n   \n   Since \\( u + v = p \\), the equation becomes:\n\n   \\[\n   \\begin{aligned}\n   u^3 + v^3 &= (u + v)(u^2 - uv + v^2) \\\\\n   &= p(u^2 - uv + v^2)\n   \\end{aligned}\n   \\]\n\n4. Also, combining the cube roots data:\n\n   \\[\n   \\begin{aligned}\n   u^3 + v^3 &= (1-x) + (1+x) \\\\\n   &= 2,\n   \\end{aligned}\n   \\]\n\n   Plugging this back into the identity gives:\n\n   \\[\n   2 = p(u^2 - uv + v^2).\n   \\]\n\n5. Next, express \\( u^2 + v^2 \\) and \\( uv \\) in terms of \\( p \\).\n\n   \\[\n   \\begin{aligned}\n   u^2 + v^2 &= (u + v)^2 - 2uv \\\\\n   &= p^2 - 2uv \\\\\n   u^2 - uv + v^2 &= (u^2 + v^2) - uv \\\\\n   &= p^2 - 3uv.\n   \\end{aligned}\n   \\]\n\n6. Substitute this back into the equation:\n\n   \\[\n   \\begin{aligned}\n   2 = p(p^2 - 3uv) \\\\\n   2 = p^3 - 3p \\cdot uv,\n   \\end{aligned}\n   \\]\n\n   Solving for \\( uv \\):\n\n   \\[\n   \\begin{aligned}\n   2 = p^3 - 3p \\cdot uv \\\\\n   3puv = p^3 - 2 \\\\\n   uv &= \\frac{p^3 - 2}{3p}.\n   \\end{aligned}\n   \\]\n\n7. Now \\( u^3 \\) and \\( v^3 \\) are roots of the quadratic equation \\( t^2 - 2t + \\left( \\frac{p^3 - 2}{3p} \\right)^3 = 0 \\).\n\n8. Solve the quadratic equation:\n\n   \\[\n   t = \\frac{2 \\pm \\sqrt{4 - 4 \\left( \\frac{p^3 - 2}{3p} \\right)^3}}{2}.\n   \\]\n\n9. Thus, \\( x \\) is given by:\n\n   \\[\n   x = \\pm \\sqrt{1 - \\left( \\frac{p^3 - 2}{3p} \\right)^3}.\n   \\]\n\n10. We need to check under which conditions \\( 1 - \\left( \\frac{p^3 - 2}{3p} \\right)^3 \\) is non-negative:\n\n    \\[\n    1 - \\frac{p^3 - 2}{3p} = \\frac{3p - (p^3 - 2)}{3p} = \\frac{-p^3 + 3p - 2}{3p} = \\frac{-(p-1)^2(p+2)}{3p}.\n    \\]\n\n11. This expression is non-negative when:\n\n    a. \\( p = -1 \\)\n    \n    b. \\( 0 < p \\leq 2 \\)\n\n12. We must check for extraneous solutions using the derived quadratic form:\n\n    \\[\n    (u + v - p)(u^2 + v^2 + p^2 + up + vp - uv) = 0.\n    \\]\n\n13. One condition is \\( u + v = p \\) which we have used; the other condition is:\n\n    \\[\n    u^2 + v^2 + p^2 - (up + vp - uv) = 0\n    \\]\n\n    which simplifies to:\n\n    \\[\n    (u + p)^2 + (v + p)^2 + (u - v)^2 = 0,\n    \\]\n\n    implying \\( u = v = -p \\), which works only when \\( p = -1 \\) and it gives \\( x = 0 \\).\n\n14. However, \\( (1 + x)^{1/3} + (1 - x)^{1/3} \\neq -1 \\) for \\( x = 0 \\).\n\n### Conclusion:\nThus, the solution is:\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{ x = \\pm \\sqrt{1 - \\left( \\frac{p^3 - 2}{3p} \\right)^3} \\quad \\text{for} \\quad 0 < p \\leq 2.}\n\\]\nFor other values of \\( p \\), there are no solutions for \\( x \\).", "domain": "math", "source": "numina_math", "test_cases": null, "starter_code": null}, {"problem": "Using a rifle, 10 bullets are shot, and for each bullseye hit, 2 additional bullets are rewarded. Using a pistol, 14 bullets are shot, and for each bullseye hit, 4 additional bullets are rewarded. Xiao Wang used a rifle, and Xiao Li used a pistol. When they have finished shooting both the original and rewarded bullets, the number of shots they each took is the same. If Xiao Wang hit the bullseye 30 times, how many times did Xiao Li hit the bullseye?", "deepseek_reasoning": "Okay, let me try to figure