2023.", "deepseek_solution": "Aline starts with 1 sheet of paper. Each time she tears one part into three, the total number of parts increases by 2. This forms an arithmetic sequence starting at 1, with a common difference of 2. Thus, after \\( n \\) tears, the total number of parts is \\( 1 + 2n \\).\n\n**For 2020 parts:**  \nSolve \\( 1 + 2n = 2020 \\):  \n\\( 2n = 2019 \\)  \n\\( n = 1009.5 \\), which is not an integer.  \n**Conclusion:** Impossible.\n\n**For 2023 parts:**  \nSolve \\( 1 + 2n = 2023 \\):  \n\\( 2n = 2022 \\)  \n\\( n = 1011 \\), an integer.  \n**Conclusion:** Possible.\n\n**Final Answer:**  \nAline cannot obtain 2020 parts, but she can obtain 2023 parts.  \n\n\\boxed{2020 \\text{ No}}  \n\\boxed{2023 \\text{ Yes}}", "ground_truth_solution": "\n1. **Initial Observation:**\n   - Aline starts with one piece of paper and tears it into three parts. Thus, she transforms 1 piece into 3 pieces.\n   - Mathematically, the first tearing process can be represented as:\n     \\[\n     1 \\rightarrow 3\n     \\]\n\n2. **First Operation Analysis:**\n   - She tears one of these three parts into three more parts, adding two new parts. \n   - Hence, the total number of parts she now has is:\n     \\[\n     3 + 2 = 5\n     \\]\n   - This can be generalized as: each operation adds two new parts to the total number of parts.\n\n3. **General Pattern and Recurrence:**\n   - Determine the change in the total number of parts with each operation:\n     - Let \\( n \\) be the current number of parts.\n     - After tearing one part into three parts, the new total number of parts is:\n       \\[\n       n + 2\n       \\]\n   - Starting from 1, each subsequent operation adds 2 parts, forming the sequence:\n     \\[\n     1, 3, 5, 7, \\ldots\n     \\]\n   - Clearly, this sequence consists only of odd numbers.\n\n4. **Reaching Specific Part Count:**\n   - To determine if Aline can ever get precisely 2020 parts:\n     - Since 2020 is an even number and our sequence includes only