}{2}} (n - 2i) \\binom{n}{i}\n\\]\n\nWe can split this summation:\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\sum_{i=0}^{\\frac{n-1}{2}} (n - 2i) \\binom{n}{i} &= n \\sum_{i=0}^{\\frac{n-1}{2}} \\binom{n}{i} - 2\\sum_{i=0}^{\\frac{n-1}{2}} i \\binom{n}{i}\n\\end{aligned}\n\\]\n\nUsing the identity from Step 1:\n\\[\n2 \\sum_{i=0}^{\\frac{n-1}{2}} i \\binom{n}{i} = 2n \\sum_{i=1}^{\\frac{n-1}{2}} \\binom{n-1}{i-1} = 2n \\sum_{i=0}^{\\frac{n-1}{2} - 1} \\binom{n-1}{i}\n\\]\n\nSubstituting the sum:\n\\[\n2 \\sum_{i=1}^{\\frac{n-1}{2}} \\binom{n-1}{i-1} = 2n (2^{n-2}) = n 2^{n-1}\n\\]\n\nThen:\n\\[\n\\sum_{i=0}^{\\frac{n-1}{2}} (n - 2i) \\binom{n}{i} = n 2^{n-1} - n 2^{n-1} = 0\n\\]\n\n#### Step 4: Consider Case \\( n \\) Even:\n\nAssume \\( n \\) is even. Following similar steps, we adjust the upper limits:\n\n\\[\n\\sum_{i=0}^{\\frac{n}{2}} (n - 2i) \\binom{n}{i} = n \\left(2^{n-1} + \\frac{1}{2} \\binom{n}{\\frac{n}{2}}\\right) - 2n \\sum_{i=0}^{\\frac{n-2}{2}} \\binom{n-1}{i}\n\\]\n\nWhere:\n\\[\nn 2^{n-1} + \\frac{n}{2} \\binom{n}{\\frac{n}{2}} - (2^{n-1} - \\binom{n-1}{\\frac{n}{2}})\n\\]\n\nCombining terms, validate we get equivalencies:\n\\[\n\\frac{1}{2} \\left( \\sum_{i=0}^{\\left\\lfloor \\frac{n}{2} \\right\\rfloor} (n - 2i) \\binom{n}{i} \\right) = \\frac{1}{2} n \\left(\\binom{n-1}{\\left\\lfloor \\frac{n}{2} \\right\\rfloor} -1 \\right)\n\\]\n\nLastly verifying factorials establish:\n\\[\n\\boxed{ \\frac{n \\left( \\left(n-1\\right) C[\\frac{n}{2}] - 1 \\right)}{2} }\n\\]", "domain": "math", "source": "numina_math", "test_cases": null, "starter_code": null}, {"problem": "The faces of a \\(9 \\times 9 \\times 9\\) cube are divided into unit cells. The cube is covered without overlaps by paper strips of size \\(2 \\times 1\\) (the sides of the strips align with the sides of the cells). Prove that the number of folded strips is odd.", "deepseek_reasoning": "Okay, so I need to prove that the number of folded strips in a 9x9x9 cube covered by 2x1 paper strips is odd. Hmm, let's