ce formula, we find the coordinates of \\( E \\) and \\( D \\) to set up the equation for \\( DE = y \\).\n   - After simplification, we derive the equation for \\( c \\) in terms of \\( y \\).\n\n4. **Expression for \\( x \\)**:\n   - Using the coordinates of \\( C \\) and the distance formula for \\( BC \\), we express \\( x^2 \\) in terms of \\( c \\).\n   - Substitute \\( c = \\frac{21(21 - y)}{2y} \\) into the expression for \\( x^2 \\).\n\n5. **Divisors and Perfect Square Check**:\n   - Determine that \\( y \\) must be a divisor of 14553 (factorization of 14553 gives possible divisors).\n   - Check possible values of \\( y \\) between 8 and 20, finding \\( y = 11 \\) as a valid solution.\n\n6. **Verification**:\n   - Substitute \\( y = 11 \\) into the expression for \\( x^2 \\), resulting in \\( x = 30 \\).\n\nThus, the value of \\( x \\) is \\(\\boxed{30}\\).", "ground_truth_solution": "\n1. **Establish Constraints and Range for \\( y \\):**\n   \n   Given the conditions \\(AD = DE = EC = y \\, \\text{cm}\\), where \\(y\\) is an integer and \\(y < 21 \\, \\text{cm}\\). We apply the triangle inequality to \\( \\triangle ADE \\):\n\n   \\[\n   AD + DE > AE \\Rightarrow y + y > 21 - y \\Rightarrow 2y > 21 - y \\Rightarrow 3y > 21 \\Rightarrow y > 7\n   \\]\n\n   Thus, \\( y \\) must satisfy:\n\n   \\[\n   8 \\leq y \\leq 20\n   \\]\n\n2. **Apply the Cosine Law:**\n\n   In \\( \\triangle ADE \\), the angle \\( \\angle A \\) is the same as in \\( \\triangle ABC \\). Using the cosine law in both triangles, we can equate the expressions for \\( \\cos A \\):\n\n   For \\( \\triangle ADE \\):\n\n   \\[\n   \\cos A = \\frac{y^2 + (21 - y)^2 - y^2}{2y(21 - y)}\n   \\]\n\n   Simplify the expression:\n\n   \\[\n   \\cos A = \\frac{(21 - y)^2}{2y(21 - y)} = \\frac{21 - y}{2y}\n   \\]\n\n   For \\( \\triangle ABC \\):\n\n   \\[\n   \\cos A = \\frac{33^2 + 21^2 - x^2}{2(33)(21)}\n   \\]\n\n   Simplify \\(33^2 + 21^2\\):\n\n   \\[\n   33^2 = 1089, \\quad 21^2 = 441 \\quad \\Rightarrow \\quad 33^2 + 21^2 = 1530\n   \\]\n\n   Then:\n\n   \\[\n   \\cos A = \\frac{1530 - x^2}{1386}\n   \\]\n\n3. **Equate the Two Expressions for \\( \\cos A \\):**\n\n   \\[\n   \\frac{21 - y}{2y} = \\frac{1530 - x^2}{1386}\n   \\]\n\n4. **Cross Multiply to Solve for \\( y \\) and \\( x^2 \\):**\n\n   \\[\n   (21 - y) \\cdot 1386 = 2y \\cdot (1530 - x^2)\n   \\]\n\n   Expand and simplify:\n\n   \\[\n   1386 \\cdot 21 - 1386 \\cdot y = 3060y - 2yx^2\n   \\]\n\n   Further simplify:\n\n   \\[\n   29106 - 1386y = 3060y - 2yx^2\n   \\]\n   \n   Combine like terms:\n\n   \\[\n   29106 = 4446y - 2yx^2\n   \\]\n   \n   Solving for \\( y \\):\n   \n   \\[\n   4446y - 2yx^2 = 29106\n   \\]\n   \n   \\[\n   2yx^2 = 4446y - 29106\n   \\]\n   \n   \\[\n   x^2 = \\frac{4446y - 29106}{2y}\n   \\]\n\n5. **Substitute Potential Values for \\( y \\):**\n\n   Since \\( 8 \\leq y \\leq 20 \\), we test \\( y = 9 \\) and \\( y = 11 \\).\n\n   - For \\( y = 9 \\):\n     \\[\n     x^2 = \\frac{4446 \\cdot 9 - 29106}{2 \\cdot 9} = \\frac{40014 - 29106}{18} = \\frac{10908}{18} \\approx 606 \\quad (\\text{not a perfect square})\n     \\]\n     \n   - For \\( y = 11 \\):\n     \\[\n     x^2 = \\frac{4446 \\cdot 11 - 29106}{2 \\cdot 11} = \\frac{48906 - 29106}{22} = \\frac{19800}{22} = 900 \\quad (\\text{a perfect square})\n     \\]\n     \n     Thus:\n     \\[\n     x = \\sqrt{900} = 30\n     \\]\n\n**Conclusion:**\n\nThe value of \\( x \\) in this scenario is \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{30}\n\\]", "domain": "math", "source": "numina_math", "test_cases": null, "starter_code": null}, {"problem": "Solve the inequalities:\n(1) $|5-x| > 6$;\n(2) $\\left|x^{2}+9 x-1\\right| \\leqslant 9$;\n(3) $3 \\leqslant |4 x-1| < 7$;\n(4) $\\left|\\frac{x+1}{x-1}\\right| \\geqslant 2$.", "deepseek_reasoning": "Okay, let me try to solve these inequalities one by one. I'll start with the first one: |5 - x| > 6. Hmm, absolute value inequalities can sometimes be tricky, but I remember that if |A| > B, then A > B or A < 