inear and show the ratio \\( \\frac{A_{2}B_{2}}{B_{2}C_{2}} \\), we start by establishing their positions relative to the incenter \\(I\\) and the orthocenter \\(H\\) of \\(\\triangle ABC\\).\n\n1. *Collinearity of \\(A_{2}, B_{2}, C_{2}\\):*\n    - We need to show that the points \\(A_{2}, B_{2}, C_{2}\\) lie on the line \\(OI\\), where \\(O\\) is the circumcenter of \\(\\triangle ABC\\) and \\(I\\) is the incenter.\n    - Assume \\(B, C\\) perpendiculars from \\(H\\) to sides \\(CA, AB\\) intersect \\(CA, AB\\) at \\(B_{3}, C_{3}\\), respectively.\n\n2. *Similarity of Triangles:*\n    - Notice that triangles \\( \\triangle ABC \\sim \\triangle AB_{3}C_{3} \\) have a similarity ratio of \\(\\cos A\\) by following the properties of the angles and lengths:\n      \\[\n      AB_{3} = AB \\cos A, \\quad AC_{3} = AC \\cos A\n      \\]\n    - Since \\( \\angle B_{3}AH = \\angle C_{3}AH = 90^\\circ \\), \\(A B_{3} H C_{3}\\) forms a cyclic quadrilateral.\n\n3. *Circumcircle of \\(\\triangle AB_{3}C_{3} \\):*\n    - The circumcenter of \\(\\triangle AB_{3}C_{3}\\) is \\(A_{1} \\), analogous to points on the lines perpendicular to \\(AH\\).\n    - Point \\(I_{A}\\) lies on the angle bisector of \\(\\angle B_{3}AC_{3} = \\angle BAC\\), therefore \\(A, I_{A}, I\\) are collinear.\n\n4. *Length Relations:*\n    - From triangle similarity:\n      \\[\n      AI_{A} = AI \\cos A\n      \\]\n    - Distance \\(II_{A}\\):\n      \\[\n      II_{A} = AI \\cdot (1 - \\cos A)\n      \\]\n    - For the incenter cuts angle:\n      \\[\n      \\angle IB_{0}C_{0} = \\frac{1}{2} \\angle A\n      \\]\n    - Hence, the distance:\n      \\[\n      ID = r \\sin \\frac{A}{2}\n      \\]\n    - where \\(r\\) is the inradius, and using the properties of the incircle:\n      \\[\n      AI = \\frac{r}{\\sin \\frac{A}{2}}\n      \\]\n    - From here:\n      \\[\n      ID = AI \\sin^{2} \\frac{A}{2} = \\frac{1}{2} AI (1 - \\cos A)\n      \\]\n    - Thus:\n      \\[\n      II_{A} = 2ID \\text{, i.e., }II_