ase, but not necessarily the orthocenter or centroid. Wait, in a right triangle pyramid with lateral edges inclined at 45 degrees, maybe the apex is projected onto the right-angle vertex? Or maybe onto another point. Let's try to visualize.\n\nWait, in order for all lateral edges to make 45 degrees with the base, their projections onto the base would be equal in length to the height of the pyramid. Because if the angle between the lateral edge and the base is 45 degrees, then tan(45\u00b0) = height / projection. But tan(45\u00b0) is 1, so height = projection. Therefore, the horizontal distance from the apex's projection to each vertex is equal to the height h.\n\nBut the apex's projection onto the base would need to be a point such that its distance to each of the three vertices of the base is equal to h. But the base is a right triangle with vertices at points A, B, C. Let's say the right angle is at point A, and the other vertices are B and C. Then, if the apex is projected onto point O on the base, then OA, OB, and OC must all be equal to h. Wait, but in a right triangle, there's no point inside the triangle that is equidistant to all three vertices except the circumradius. Wait, the circumradius of a right triangle is half the hypotenuse. So in this case, the circumradius is c/2. Therefore, the point equidistant to all three vertices is the midpoint of the hypotenuse, which is at a distance of c/2 from each vertex. So if that's the case, then h = c/2. But is that the case here?\n\nWait, no. Because the projection of the apex onto the base must be a point such that the distance from that point to each vertex is equal to h. Because if the lateral edges are inclined at 45 degrees, then the horizontal component (the projection) is equal to the vertical component (the height). So, if the apex is at height h, then the horizontal distance from the projection to each vertex is h. So the projection point O must be at distance h from each vertex. Therefore, O must be equi