ge as \\( x \\).\n\n2. According to the problem, Dad is only counting weekdays (Monday to Friday), which are \\(\\frac{5}{7}\\) of the week. So, the age he reports is \\(\\frac{5}{7}\\) of his true age.\n\n3. Mathematically, we can express this relationship as: \n   \\[\n   \\frac{5}{7} \\times x = 35\n   \\]\n\n4. To solve for \\( x \\), we need to isolate \\( x \\). We can do this by multiplying both sides of the equation by the reciprocal of \\(\\frac{5}{7}\\), which is \\(\\frac{7}{5}\\):\n   \\[\n   x = 35 \\times \\frac{7}{5}\n   \\]\n\n5. Perform the multiplication:\n   \\[\n   x = 35 \\times \\frac{7}{5} = 35 \\times 1.4 = 49\n   \\]\n\n6. Therefore, Dad's real age is:\n   \\(\\boxed{49}\\)\n\nThe correct answer is \\(\\boxed{D}\\).", "domain": "math", "source": "numina_math", "test_cases": null, "starter_code": null}, {"problem": "Prove that for \\( n \\geq 5 \\), the cross-section of a pyramid, whose base is a regular \\( n \\)-gon, cannot be a regular \\( (n+1) \\)-gon.", "deepseek_reasoning": "Okay, so I need to prove that for n \u2265 5, the cross-section of a pyramid with a regular n-gon base can't be a regular (n+1)-gon. Hmm, let me start by visualizing this. A pyramid with a regular n-gon base would have a pointy top (the apex) connected to each vertex of the base. A cross-section is a slice made by cutting the pyramid with a plane. The claim is that such a slice can't result in a regular polygon with one more side than the base. \n\nFirst, let me recall some properties. A regular n-gon has all sides equal and all internal angles equal. For a cross-section to be a regular (n+1)-gon, it must also have all sides and angles equal. Since the pyramid has an n-gon base, the cross-section would have to intersect n edges of the pyramid (each connecting the apex to a base vertex) plus possibly some other edges or the base itself. Wait, but if the cross-section is a (n+1)-gon, which has one more side than the base, how does that happen? The base is an n-gon, s