acent to four regions. Then, in this case, the outer regions form a cycle of four. Coloring such a map: the center would need one color, and the outer regions can be colored alternately with two colors if the cycle is even. Since four is even, they can be colored with two colors. So total colors used: three. Wait, but if the outer cycle is even, two colors suffice. If it's odd, three colors. But in this case, the center is adjacent to all four outer regions, so the center's color must be different from all four outer colors. If the outer cycle is colored with two colors (since four is even), then the center needs a third color. So total three colors. Hmm. So where does four come in?\n\nMaybe another example. Consider a map where a country is surrounded by five others, each of which also borders two of their neighbors. Like a pentagon around a central country. In this case, the five outer countries form a cycle of five. Coloring this cycle would require three colors because it's an odd cycle. Then the central country, which borders all five, needs a fourth color. So in this case, four colors are needed. Let me check that. If the five outer countries form a pentagon, each adjacent to two neighbors and the center. Coloring the pentagon: since it's a cycle of five (odd), you can't color it with two colors. So you need three. Let's say colors 1, 2, 3, 1, 2 around the pentagon. Then the center country can't be any of 1, 2, or 3 because it's adjacent to all five. So it needs a fourth color. Therefore, this map requires four colors. So this example shows that four colors are necessary. Therefore, the minimum number is four.\n\nBut wait, could there be a way to color the center with one of the existing colors? If the outer cycle uses three colors, is there a color not used by all five? Since there are three colors in the cycle, each color is used at least once, maybe twice. But the center is adjacent to all five, each of which has one of three colors. So all three colors are