ncy of Four Colors**:  \n   The Four Color Theorem (proven in 1976) guarantees that four colors are always enough to color any planar map without adjacent regions sharing the same color. This applies even to complex maps with intricate borders.\n\n3. **Practical Implications**:  \n   While simpler maps (e.g., bipartite graphs) may only need two colors, and others (e.g., odd cycles) require three, the theorem ensures that **four colors universally suffice** for all cases.\n\n**Final Answer**:  \nThe minimum number of colors required is **4**, and this number is always sufficient for any map.", "ground_truth_solution": "\n1. **Problem Restatement:**\n\n   We need to determine the minimum number of colors required to color a geographic (or political) map such that no two adjacent regions (countries or provinces) share the same color. Additionally, we need to confirm if this number is always sufficient.\n\n2. **Key Concept:**\n   \n   The problem can be related to graph theory, where the map is represented as a graph. Each region is a vertex, and each border between two regions is an edge. The problem of coloring the map corresponds to the problem of vertex coloring in a graph.\n\n3. **Four Color Theorem:**\n\n   The Four Color Theorem states that four colors are sufficient to color any map (or equivalently, any planar graph) such that no two adjacent regions share the same color.\n\n   Explicitly, the theorem can be stated as follows:\n   \n   **Theorem (Four Color Theorem):** Any planar graph can be colored with no more than four different colors, such that no two adjacent vertices (regions) share the same color.\n\n4. **Application:**\n   \n   In our context, the map is a planar graph by definition (since it can be drawn on a plane without edges crossing). According to the Four Color Theorem, we can color each map using at most four colors.\n\n5. **Sufficiency:**\n   \n   The Four Color Theorem also assures us that four colors are always sufficient for this purpose. 