1/4)(x + 1)cos4x + (1/4)\u222bcos4x dx.\n\nSo putting it back into our previous expression, the integral becomes:\n\n-4 [ -(1/4)(x + 1)cos4x + (1/4)\u222bcos4x dx ] evaluated from 0 to \u03c0.\n\nLet me distribute the -4:\n\n-4 * [ -(1/4)(x + 1)cos4x ] becomes -4 * (-1/4) (x + 1)cos4x = (x + 1)cos4x.\n\nThen the other term is -4 * (1/4) \u222bcos4x dx = -1 \u222bcos4x dx. The integral of cos4x is (1/4) sin4x, so that term becomes -1*(1/4 sin4x) = - (1/4) sin4x.\n\nSo putting it all together, the integral is:\n\n[ (x + 1)cos4x - (1/4) sin4x ] evaluated from 0 to \u03c0.\n\nNow let's compute this at the limits \u03c0 and 0.\n\nFirst, at x = \u03c0:\n\n(\u03c0 + 1)cos(4\u03c0) - (1/4) sin(4\u03c0). cos4\u03c0 is cos(0) since 4\u03c0 is a multiple of 2\u03c0. cos4\u03c0 = 1. sin4\u03c0 is 0. So this becomes (\u03c0 + 1)(1) - (1/4)(0) = \u03c0 + 1.\n\nAt x = 0:\n\n(0 + 1)cos0 - (1/4) sin0. cos0 = 1, sin0 = 0. So this is 1*1 - 0 = 1.\n\nTherefore, the integral from 0 to \u03c0 is [\u03c0 + 1] - [1] = \u03c0 + 1 - 1 = \u03c0.\n\nBut wait, let's make sure we didn't make a mistake in signs. Let's retrace the steps.\n\nOriginal integral: \u222b0 to \u03c0 (8x\u00b2 +16x +17)cos4x dx.\n\nFirst integration by parts:\n\nu = 8x\u00b2 +16x +17, dv = cos4x dx.\n\ndu = (16x +16) dx, v = (1/4) sin4x.\n\nSo integral becomes uv|0 to \u03c0 - \u222b0 to \u03c0 v du.\n\nuv evaluated at \u03c0: (8\u03c0\u00b2 +16\u03c0 +17)(1/4 sin4\u03c0) = 0.\n\nuv at 0: (0 + 0 +17)(1/4 *0) =0.\n\nSo first term is 0 -0=0.\n\nThen - \u222b0 to \u03c0 (1/4 sin4x)(16x +16) dx.\n\nFactor out 16 and 1/4: - (16/4) \u222b0 to \u03c0 (x +1) sin4x dx = -4 \u222b(x +1) sin4x dx.\n\nThen, integration by parts again on \u222b(x +1) sin4x dx.\n\nu = x +1, du = dx. dv = sin4x dx, v= - (1/4) cos4x.\n\nSo integral becomes uv - \u222bv du = - (1/4)(x +1) cos4x + (1/4) \u222bcos4x dx.\n\nThus, -4 times that is:\n\n-4 [ - (1/4)(x +1)cos4x + (1/4)(1/4 sin4x) ]\n\nWait, hold on, in my previous step, I think I missed a step. Let me correct this.\n\nAfter integrating by parts the second time, the integral \u222b(x +1) sin4x dx becomes:\n\n- (1/4)(x +1)cos4x + (1/4) \u222bcos4x dx.\n\nSo the integral of cos4x is (1/4) sin4x. Therefore, the expression becomes:\n\n- (1/4)(x +1)cos4x + (1/4)*(1/4) sin4x = - (1/4)(x +1)cos4x + (1/16) sin4x.\n\nThen, multiplying by -4:\n\n-4 [ - (1/4)(x +1)cos4x + (1/16) sin4x ] = -4*(-1/4)(x +1)cos4x + (-4)*(1/16) sin4x.\n\nSimplify each term:\n\nFirst term: (-4)*(-1/4) = 1, so it becomes (x +1)cos4x.\n\nSecond term: (-4)*(1/16) = -1/4, so it becomes - (1/4) sin4x.\n\nSo the entire expression is (x +1)cos4x - (1/4) sin4x evaluated from 0 to \u03c0.\n\nSo evaluating at \u03c0:\n\n(\u03c0 +1)cos4\u03c0 - (1/4) sin4\u03c0. cos4\u03c0 is 1, sin4\u03c0 is 0. So this term is (\u03c0 +1)(1) - 0 = \u03c0 +1.\n\nAt x=0:\n\n(0 +1)cos0 - (1/4) sin0 = 1*1 - 0 =1.\n\nSubtracting, we get (\u03c0 +1) -1 = \u03c0.\n\nTherefore, the value of the integral is \u03c0.\n\nWait, but let me check if I missed any negative signs. Let's double-check.\n\nOriginal integral: After first integration by parts, we had -4 \u222b(x +1) sin4x dx.\n\nThen, integrating \u222b(x +1) sin4x dx = - (1/4)(x +1)cos4x + (1/4)\u222bcos4x dx.\n\nThen, multiplying by -4:\n\n-4 [ - (1/4)(x +1)cos4x + (1/16) sin4x ]\n\nWhich is (x +1)cos4x - (1/4) sin4x.\n\nEvaluated from 0 to \u03c0: [(\u03c0 +1)(1) - 0] - [1 -0] = \u03c0 +1 -1 = \u03c0. That seems correct.\n\nSo the final answer is \u03c0. But let me check with another method or verify with differentiation.\n\nAlternatively, maybe I can compute the integral step by step again to confirm.\n\nAlternatively, suppose I consider integrating (8x\u00b2 +16x +17)cos4x dx.\n\nAlternatively, use tabular integration since it's a polynomial times a trigonometric function.\n\nSet up a table:\n\nDifferentiate the polynomial part: 8x\u00b2 +16x +17, then 16x +16, then 16, then 0.\n\nIntegrate cos4x: (1/4) sin4x, then (-1/16) cos4x, then (-1/64) sin4x, then (1/256) cos4x.\n\nMultiply diagonally, alternating signs:\n\nFirst term: (8x\u00b2 +16x +17)(1/4 sin4x)\n\nSecond term: - (16x +16)(-1/16 cos4x) = (16x +16)(1/16 cos4x) = (x +1)cos4x\n\nThird term: + (16)(-1/64 sin4x) = -16/64 sin4x = - (1/4) sin4x\n\nFourth term would be 0 times something, so stops here.\n\nSo the integral is:\n\n(8x\u00b2 +16x +17)(1/4 sin4x) + (x +1)cos4x - (1/4) sin4x evaluated from 0 to \u03c0.\n\nNow, evaluating at \u03c0:\n\nFirst term: (8\u03c0\u00b2 +16\u03c0 +17)(1/4 sin4\u03c0) = 0.\n\nSecond term: (\u03c0 +1)cos4\u03c0 = (\u03c0 +1)(1).\n\nThird term: - (1/4) sin4\u03c0 = 0.\n\nSo total at \u03c0: \u03c0 +1.\n\nAt x=0:\n\nFirst term: (0 +0 +17)(1/4 sin0) =0.\n\nSecond term: (0 +1)cos0 =1*1=1.\n\nThird term: - (1/4) sin0 =0.\n\nTotal at 0:1.\n\nSo subtracting, \u03c0 +1 -1 = \u03c0. Same result.\n\nTherefore, the integral is indeed \u03c0. So the answer is \u03c0.\n\nBut wait, is there any chance I missed a coefficient? Let me check with another approach.\n\nAlternatively, suppose I expand the original integral:\n\n\u222b (8x\u00b2 +16x +17) cos4x dx.\n\nWe can split into three integrals:\n\n8\u222bx\u00b2 cos4x dx +16\u222bx cos4x dx +17\u222bcos4x dx.\n\nCompute each separately.\n\nFirst integral: 8\u222bx\u00b2 cos4x dx.\n\nSecond: 16\u222bx cos4x dx.\n\nThird: 17\u222bcos4x dx.\n\nCompute each part.\n\nStarting with \u222bx\u00b2 cos4x dx.\n\nIntegration by parts, let u =x\u00b2, dv=cos4x dx.\n\ndu=2x dx, v=(1/4) sin4x.\n\nSo uv - \u222bv du = x\u00b2*(1/4 sin4x) - \u222b(1/4 sin4x)*2x dx = (x\u00b2/4) sin4x - (1/2) \u222bx sin4x dx.\n\nNow, \u222bx sin4x dx needs another integration by parts.\n\nLet u =x, dv=sin4x dx.\n\ndu=dx, v= - (1/4) cos4x.\n\nSo uv - \u222bv du = -x/(4) cos4x + (1/4) \u222bcos4x dx = -x/4 cos4x + (1/4)*(1/4 sin4x) = -x/4 cos4x + (1/16) sin4x.\n\nThus, going back to the first integral:\n\n8 [ (x\u00b2/4) sin4x - (1/2)( -x/4 cos4x + 1/16 sin4x ) ] = 8*(x\u00b2/4 sin4x + x/8 cos4x - 1/32 sin4x )\n\nSimplify:\n\n8*(x\u00b2/4) = 2x\u00b2 sin4x,\n\n8*(x/8) = x cos4x,\n\n8*(-1/32) = -1/4 sin4x.\n\nSo first integral: 2x\u00b2 sin4x + x cos4x - (1/4) sin4x.\n\nSecond integral: 16\u222bx cos4x dx.\n\nAgain, integration by parts.\n\nu =x, dv=cos4x dx.\n\ndu=dx, v=(1/4) sin4x.\n\nuv - \u222bv du = x*(1/4 sin4x) - \u222b(1/4 sin4x) dx = (x/4) sin4x + (1/16) cos4x.\n\nMultiply by 16:\n\n16*(x/4 sin4x + 1/16 cos4x) =4x sin4x + cos4x.\n\nThird integral: 17\u222bcos4x dx =17*(1/4 sin4x) = (17/4) sin4x.\n\nNow, combine all three integrals:\n\nFirst integral: 2x\u00b2 sin4x + x cos4x - (1/4) sin4x.\n\nSecond integral:4x sin4x + cos4x.\n\nThird integral: (17/4) sin4x.\n\nSum all together:\n\n2x\u00b2 sin4x +x cos4x - (1/4) sin4x +4x sin4x +cos4x + (17/4) sin4x.\n\nCombine like terms:\n\nsin4x terms:\n\n2x\u00b2 sin4x +4x sin4x + (-1/4 +17/4) sin4x =2x\u00b2 sin4x +4x sin4x + (16/4) sin4x =2x\u00b2 sin4x +4x sin4x +4 sin4x.\n\ncos4x terms:\n\nx cos4x + cos4x.\n\nSo overall, the antiderivative is:\n\n2x\u00b2 sin4x +4x sin4x +4 sin4x +x cos4x +cos4x.\n\nFactor where possible:\n\nsin4x terms: sin4x (2x\u00b2 +4x +4) + cos4x (x +1).\n\nTherefore, the antiderivative is sin4x (2x\u00b2 +4x +4) + cos4x (x +1).\n\nNow, evaluate from 0 to \u03c0.\n\nAt x=\u03c0:\n\nsin4\u03c0 (2\u03c0\u00b2 +4\u03c0 +4) + cos4\u03c0 (\u03c0 +1).\n\nsin4\u03c0 is 0, so first term is 0. cos4\u03c0 is 1, so second term is (\u03c0 +1)*1 = \u03c0 +1.\n\nAt x=0:\n\nsin0 (0 +0 +4) + cos0 (0 +1). sin0 is 0, first term 0. cos0 is 1, second term is 1*1=1.\n\nSubtracting, the integral is (\u03c0 +1) -1 = \u03c0.\n\nSo this confirms the result again. Therefore, the definite integral equals \u03c0.\n\n**Final Answer**\nThe value of the definite integral is \\boxed{\\pi}.", "deepseek_solution": "To calculate the definite integral:\n\n$$\n\\int_{0}^{\\pi}\\left(8 x^{2}+16 x+17\\right) \\cos 4 x \\,d x\n$$\n\nwe use integration by parts. Let \\( u = 8x^2 + 16x + 17 \\) and \\( dv = \\cos 4x \\, dx \\). Then, \\( du = (16x + 16) \\, dx \\) and \\( v = \\frac{1}{4} \\sin 4x \\). Applying integration by parts:\n\n$$\n\\int u \\, dv = uv - \\int v \\, du\n$$\n\nEvaluating the boundary term \\( uv \\) from 0 to \\(\\pi\\) results in zero because \\(\\sin 4\\pi = 0\\) and \\(\\sin 0 = 0\\). This leaves us with:\n\n$$\n- \\int_{0}^{\\pi} \\frac{1}{4} \\sin 4x \\cdot (16x + 16) \\, dx = -4 \\int_{0}^{\\pi} (x + 1) \\sin 4x \\, dx\n$$\n\nWe apply integration by parts again to the remaining integral. Let \\( u = x + 1 \\) and \\( dv = \\sin 4x \\, dx \\). Then, \\( du = dx \\) and \\( v = -\\frac{1}{4} \\cos 4x \\):\n\n$$\n-4 \\left( -\\frac{1}{4} (x + 1) \\cos 4x + \\frac{1}{4} \\int \\cos 4x \\, dx \\right)\n$$\n\nSimplifying, we get:\n\n$$\n-4 \\left( -\\frac{1}{4} (x + 1) \\cos 4x + \\frac{1}{16} \\sin 4x \\right) = (x + 1) \\cos 4x - \\frac{1}{4} \\sin 4x\n$$\n\nEvaluating this from 0 to \\(\\pi\\):\n\nAt \\( x = \\pi \\):\n\n$$\n(\\pi + 1) \\cos 4\\pi - \\frac{1}{4} \\sin 4\\pi = (\\pi + 1) \\cdot 1 - 0 = \\pi + 1\n$$\n\nAt \\( x = 0 \\):\n\n$$\n(0 + 1) \\cos 0 - \\frac{1}{4} \\sin 0 = 1 \\cdot 1 - 0 = 1\n$$\n\nSubtracting these results:\n\n$$\n(\\pi + 1) - 1 = \\pi\n$$\n\nThus, the value of the definite integral is:\n\n$$\n\\boxed{\\pi}\n```", "ground_truth_solution": "\nWe need to compute the definite integral:\n\n$$\n\\int_{0}^{\\pi} \\left(8x^2 + 16x + 17\\right) \\cos 4x \\, dx\n$$\n\n#### Step 1: Using Integration by Parts\n\nIntegration by parts is given by the formula:\n\n$$\n\\int u \\, dv = uv - \\int v \\, du\n$$\n\nLet's choose:\n\\[ \nu = 8x^2 + 16x + 17 \\quad \\text{and} \\quad dv = \\cos 4x \\, dx \n\\]\n\nThen,\n\\[ \ndu = (16x + 16) \\, dx \\quad \\text{and} \\quad v = \\frac{1}{4} \\sin 4x \n\\]\n\nApplying integration by parts:\n\n$$\n\\int_{0}^{\\pi} \\left(8x^2 + 16x + 17\\right) \\cos 4x \\, dx = \\left. \\left(8x^2 + 16x + 17\\right) \\cdot \\frac{1}{4} \\sin 4x \\right|_{0}^{\\pi} - \\int_{0}^{\\pi} \\frac{1}{4} \\sin 4x \\cdot (16x + 16) \\, dx \n$$\n\n#### Step 2: Evaluating the Boundary Terms\n\nEvaluating the first term at \\( x = \\pi \\) and \\( x = 0 \\):\n\n\\[ \n\\left. \\left(8x^2 + 16x + 17\\right) \\cdot \\frac{1}{4} \\sin 4x \\right|_{0}^{\\pi} = \\left. \\left(8\\pi^2 + 16\\pi + 17\\right) \\cdot \\frac{1}{4} \\sin (4\\pi) \\right| - \\left. \\left(8 \\cdot 0^2 + 16 \\cdot 0 + 17\\right) \\cdot \\frac{1}{4} \\sin (0) \\right|\n\\]\n\nRecall that \\( \\sin(4\\pi) = 0 \\) and \\( \\sin(0) = 0 \\):\n\n\\[ \n\\left(8\\pi^2 + 16\\pi + 17\\right) \\cdot 0 - 17 \\cdot 0 = 0 \n\\]\n\n#### Step 3: Simplifying the Integral\n\nWe are left with the integral:\n\n\\[ \n- \\int_{0}^{\\pi} \\frac{1}{4} \\sin 4x \\cdot (16x + 16) \\, dx = - \\int_{0}^{\\pi} \\sin 4x \\cdot (4x + 4) \\, dx\n\\]\n\n#### Step 4: Applying Integration by Parts Again\n\nUse integration by parts:\n\\[ \nu = 4x + 4 \\quad \\text{and} \\quad dv = \\sin 4x \\, dx \n\\]\n\nThen, \n\\[ \ndu = 4 \\, dx \\quad \\text{and} \\quad v = -\\frac{1}{4} \\cos 4x \n\\]\n\nApplying integration by parts again:\n\n\\[ \n- \\int_{0}^{\\pi} \\sin 4x \\cdot (4x + 4) \\, dx = -\\left. (4x + 4) \\cdot \\left(-\\frac{1}{4} \\cos 4x\\right) \\right|_{0}^{\\pi} + \\int_{0}^{\\pi} \\left( -\\frac{1}{4} \\cos 4x \\cdot 4 \\right) \\, dx\n\\]\n\nSimplifying the expression:\n\n\\[ \n= -\\left( - (4\\pi + 4) \\cdot \\frac{1}{4} \\cos (4\\pi) + (4 \\cdot 0 + 4) \\cdot \\frac{1}{4} \\cos (0) \\right) + \\int_{0}^{\\pi} \\cos 4x \\, dx \n\\]\n\nSince \\( \\cos(4\\pi) = 1 \\) and \\( \\cos(0) = 1 \\):\n\n\\[ \n= -\\left( - (\\pi + 1) + 1 \\right) - \\int_{0}^{\\pi} \\cos 4x \\, dx = -\\left(\\pi + 1 - 1 \\right) - \\int_{0}^{\\pi} \\cos 4x \\, dx = -(-\\pi) - \\int_{0}^{\\pi} \\cos 4x \\, dx \n\\]\n\n#### Step 5: Evaluating the Remaining Cosine Integral\n\nFinally,\n\n\\[ \n\\int_{0}^{\\pi} \\cos 4x \\, dx \n\\]\n\nThe integral of \\( \\cos 4x \\) from 0 to \\( \\pi \\) is:\n\n\\[ \n\\left. \\frac{1}{4} \\sin 4x \\right|_{0}^{\\pi} = \\frac{1}{4} \\left( \\sin (4\\pi) - \\sin (0) \\right) = \\frac{1}{4} \\cdot 0 = 0\n\\]\n\nPutting it all together:\n\n\\[ \n-\\int_{0}^{\\pi} \\left(8x^2 + 16x + 17\\right) \\cos 4x \\, dx = -\\left( -\\pi \\right) = \\pi \n\\]\n\nThus, the value of the definite integral is:\n\n$$ \\boxed{\\pi} $$", "domain": "math", "source": "numina_math", "test_cases": null, "starter_code": null}, {"problem": "Triangle \\( PQR \\) is equilateral. A semicircle with center \\( O \\) is drawn with its diameter on \\( PR \\) so that one end is at \\( P \\) and the curved edge touches \\( QR \\) at \\( X \\). The radius of the semicircle is \\( \\sqrt{3} \\).\nWhat is the length of \\( QX \\)?\n\nA. \\( \\sqrt{3} \\)\n\nB. \\( 2 - \\sqrt{3} \\)\n\nC. \\( 2 \\sqrt{3} - 1 \\)\n\nD. \\( 1 + \\sqrt{3} \\)\n\nE. \\( 2 \\sqrt{3} \\)", "deepseek_reasoning": "Okay, let's tackle this geometry problem step by step. First, let me make sure I understand the problem correctly. We have an equilateral triangle PQR. A semicircle is drawn with its diameter on PR, starting at point P, and the center is O. The curved edge of the semicircle touches QR at point X, and the radius of the semicircle is \u221a3. We need to find the length of QX from the options given.\n\nSince the triangle is equilateral, all sides are equal, and all angles are 60 degrees. The semicircle is on PR, which is one side of the triangle. The semicircle touches QR at X, which suggests that X is a point of tangency. Since the radius is \u221a3, the diameter PR would be twice that, so 2\u221a3. But wait, if the triangle is equilateral, then all sides should be equal. So PR is a side of the triangle, which is 2\u221a3. Therefore, each side of the triangle PQR is 2\u221a3. That makes sense because in an equilateral triangle, all sides are the same length.\n\nNow, the semicircle is centered at O, which is the midpoint of PR because the diameter is PR. So O is at the center of PR. Therefore, the coordinates of O can be determined if we assign coordinates to the triangle. Maybe coordinate geometry can help he