es $BC$, $AC$, and $AB$, respectively.\n\n2. **Angles Analysis**:\n    - Observe that the quadrilateral $A_1BC_1I$ is inscribed in a circle, which means that opposite angles sum up to $180^\\circ$. \n    - Consequently, we have:\n      \\[\n      \\angle A_1I C_1 = 180^\\circ - \\angle B\n      \\]\n    - Similarly, since $A_2$, $B_2$, and $C_2$ are the points of tangency, we have:\n      \\[\n      \\angle A_2I C_2 = \\angle A_1I C_1\n      \\]\n    - From this, we conclude:\n      \\[\n      \\angle A_1IC_1 = \\angle A_2IC_2\n      \\]\n\n3. **Triangles Equality (Right Triangles)**:\n    - The right triangles $A_1A_2I$ and $C_1C_2I$ are congruent by RHS (Right-angle-Hypotenuse-Side), since they share a right angle at $A_2$ and $C_2$, and $\\angle A_1IC_1 = \\angle A_2IC_2$.\n    - Since one of these triangles lies inside quadrilateral $BA_2IC_2$ and the other lies outside:\n      \\[\n      AC_1 + CA_1 = AC_2 + CA_2 = AB_2 + CB_2 = AC\n      \\]\n\n4. **Constructing Parallelograms**:\n    - We form a parallelogram $AC_1KD$ and another parallelogram $CA_1KE$ such that $K$ is the midpoint of $A_1C_1$:\n    - Then, $ADCE$ also forms a parallelogram (it could potentially be degenerate but still a parallelogram), with $M$ being its center, implying that $M$ is the midpoint of $DE$.\n\n5. **Median Property**:\n    - According to problem 55150, the median ($KM$) of a triangle is less than half the perimeter of the two sides it is parallel to:\n      \\[\n      KM < \\frac{1}{2} (KD + KE)\n      \\]\n    - Since $K$ is alongside $A_1$ and $C_1$:\n      \\[\n      KD = AC_1 \\quad \\text{and} \\quad KE = CA_1, \\quad \\text{we have}\\:\n      \\]\n      \\[\n      KM < \\frac{1}{2} (AC_1 + CA_1) = \\frac{1}{2} AC\n      \\]\n\n6. **Locating Point $K$ Inside the Circle**:\n      - The point $K$ is inside the circle with diameter $AC$, thus realizing that the angle:\n    \\[\n    \\angle AKC \\quad \\text{is obtuse}.\n    \\]\n\n### Conclusion:\nThus, the angle at $K$ in tri