rac{\\vec{b} + \\vec{c}}{2} + \\frac{\\vec{a} + \\vec{c}}{2} + \\frac{\\vec{a} + \\vec{b}}{2}.\n     \\]\n   - Combine terms in the numerator:\n     \\[\n     = \\frac{ (\\vec{b} + \\vec{c}) + (\\vec{a} + \\vec{c}) + (\\vec{a} + \\vec{b}) }{2} = \\frac{2\\vec{a} + 2\\vec{b} + 2\\vec{c}}{2}.\n     \\]\n   - Simplify:\n     \\[\n     = \\vec{a} + \\vec{b} + \\vec{c}.\n     \\]\n\n3. **Compare to the Sum of Vertex Vectors**:\n   - The sum \\( \\vec{a} + \\vec{b} + \\vec{c} \\) is precisely \\( \\overrightarrow{OA} + \\overrightarrow{OB} + \\overrightarrow{OC} \\).\n\n**Conclusion**:\nThe equality holds for any point \\( O \\), as the sum of the vectors to the midpoints simplifies directly to the sum of the vectors to the vertices. This result is confirmed both through vector algebra and coordinate analysis.\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\overrightarrow{O A_{1}} + \\overrightarrow{O B_{1}} + \\overrightarrow{O C_{1}} = \\overrightarrow{O A} + \\overrightarrow{O B} + \\overrightarrow{O C}}\n\\]", "ground_truth_solution": "1. Given points \\( A_1 \\), \\( B_1 \\), and \\( C_1 \\) are the midpoints of the sides \\( BC \\), \\( AC \\), and \\( AB \\) of triangle \\( ABC \\) respectively. We need to prove that for any point \\( O \\), the equality \n\\[\n\\overrightarrow{O A_{1}} + \\overrightarrow{O B_{1}} + \\overrightarrow{O C_{1}} = \\overrightarrow{O A} + \\overrightarrow{O B} + \\overrightarrow{O C}\n\\]\nholds.\n\n2. Start by considering the vector subtraction for midpoints. For instance, for \\( A_1 \\):\n\\[\n\\overrightarrow{O A_{1}} - \\overrightarrow{O A} = \\overrightarrow{A A_{1}}\n\\]\n\n3. Express the given condition with all points:\n\\[\n\\left( \\overrightarrow{O A_{1}} + \\overrightarrow{O B_{1}} + \\overrightarrow{O C_{1}} \\right) - \\left( \\overrightarrow{O A} + \\overrightarrow{O B} + \\overrightarrow{O C} \\right)\n\\]\n\n4. Split into individual differences:\n\\[\n\\left( \\overrightarrow{O A_{1}} - \\overrightarrow{O A} \\right) + \\left( \\overrightarrow{O B_