(\u03c0/A) cos\u00b3(\u03c0/A) = 16 sin(\u03c0/A) cos\u00b3(\u03c0/A).\n\nHmm, okay. So the area is 16 sin(\u03c0/A) cos\u00b3(\u03c0/A). Let's see if we can simplify this expression further. Maybe using double-angle identities or power-reduction.\n\nAlternatively, express everything in terms of sin(2\u03c0/A) or another angle. Let me think. Alternatively, note that sin(\u03c0/A) cos\u00b3(\u03c0/A) can be written as (1/4) sin(2\u03c0/A) [1 + cos(2\u03c0/A)].\n\nWait, let's recall that sin\u03b8 cos\u00b3\u03b8 = (sin\u03b8 cos\u03b8) cos\u00b2\u03b8 = (1/2 sin2\u03b8) cos\u00b2\u03b8. Then, cos\u00b2\u03b8 can be expressed as (1 + cos2\u03b8)/2. Therefore:\n\nsin\u03b8 cos\u00b3\u03b8 = (1/2 sin2\u03b8) * (1 + cos2\u03b8)/2 = (1/4) sin2\u03b8 (1 + cos2\u03b8).\n\nTherefore, Area = 16 * (1/4) sin(2\u03c0/A) (1 + cos(2\u03c0/A)) = 4 sin(2\u03c0/A) (1 + cos(2\u03c0/A)).\n\nSimplify further. Let's compute sin(2\u03c0/A)(1 + cos(2\u03c0/A)). Recall that 1 + cos(2\u03c0/A) = 2 cos\u00b2(\u03c0/A). So:\n\nsin(2\u03c0/A) * 2 cos\u00b2(\u03c0/A) = 2 sin(2\u03c0/A) cos\u00b2(\u03c0/A).\n\nTherefore, Area = 4 * 2 sin(2\u03c0/A) cos\u00b2(\u03c0/A) = 8 sin(2\u03c0/A) cos\u00b2(\u03c0/A).\n\nBut maybe another approach. Alternatively, using the identity sin(2\u03b8) = 2 sin\u03b8 cos\u03b8, so sin(2\u03c0/A) = 2 sin(\u03c0/A) cos(\u03c0/A). Then:\n\nArea = 16 sin(\u03c0/A) cos\u00b3(\u03c0/A) = 16 sin(\u03c0/A) cos(\u03c0/A) * cos\u00b2(\u03c0/A) = 8 * 2 sin(\u03c0/A) cos(\u03c0/A) * cos\u00b2(\u03c0/A) = 8 sin(2\u03c0/A) cos\u00b2(\u03c0/A).\n\nSame as before. Maybe we can write this in terms of sin(4\u03c0/A) using double-angle identities. For example, sin(4\u03c0/A) = 2 sin(2\u03c0/A) cos(2\u03c0/A). So if we can relate the expression to sin(4\u03c0/A).\n\nBut perhaps we need to find a way to express this area in terms of radicals or known quantities so that it can be expressed as a + sqrt(b). Alternatively, maybe we can find specific values of A that make the expression 16 s