side lengths of the rectangle \\(A B G H\\) are \\(12 \\text{ cm}\\) and \\(7 \\text{ cm}\\).\n\n\\(\\boxed{12 \\text{ cm} \\text{ and } 7 \\text{ cm}}\\)", "domain": "math", "source": "numina_math", "test_cases": null, "starter_code": null}, {"problem": "Given that the center of the ellipse is at the origin $O$, the foci are on the $x$-axis, and a line with slope $\\frac{1}{2}$ passes through the right focus $F$ of the ellipse and intersects the ellipse at points $A$ and $B$. The vectors $\\overrightarrow{O A}+\\overrightarrow{O B}$ and $(-3, 1)$ are collinear.\n(1) Find the eccentricity of the ellipse;\n(2) Let $M$ be a point on the ellipse, and\n$$\n\\overrightarrow{O M}=\\lambda \\overrightarrow{O A}+\\mu \\overrightarrow{O B}(\\lambda, \\mu \\in \\mathbf{R}),\n$$\n\nprove that $\\lambda^{2}+\\mu^{2}$ is a constant.", "deepseek_reasoning": "Okay, so I have this problem about an ellipse centered at the origin with foci on the x-axis. A line with slope 1/2 passes through the right focus F and intersects the ellipse at points A and B. Also, the vector OA + OB is collinear with (-3, 1). I need to find the eccentricity of the ellipse first, and then prove that for a point M on the ellipse expressed as a linear combination of OA and OB, \u03bb\u00b2 + \u03bc\u00b2 is a constant.\n\nLet me start with part (1). Since the ellipse is centered at the origin with foci on the x-axis, its standard equation should be (x\u00b2/a\u00b2) + (y\u00b2/b\u00b2) = 1, where a is the semi-major axis, b is the semi-minor axis, and the distance from the center to each focus is c, with c\u00b2 = a\u00b2 - b\u00b2. The eccentricity e is c/a, so we need to find e = c/a.\n\nGiven that the line with slope 1/2 passes through the right focus F, which is at (c, 0). So the equation of the line is y = (1/2)(x - c). This line intersects the ellipse at points A and B. Let me denote points A and B as (x\u2081, y\u2081) and (x\u2082, y\u2082) respectively.\n\nThe vectors OA + OB would be (x\u2081 + x\u2082, y\u2081 + y\u2082). According to the problem, this vector is collinear with (-3, 1), which means there exists some scalar k such that (x\u2081 + x\u2082, y\u2081 + y\u2082) = k(-3, 1). Therefore, x\u2081 + x\u2082 = -3k and y\u2081 + y\u2082 = k. Also, since they are collinear, the ratio (x\u2081 + x\u2082)/(-3) = (y\u2081 + y\u2082)/1, so that relationship must hold.\n\nSo, first, I need to find the coordinates of points A and B where the line intersects the ellipse. Let me substitute y = (1/2)(x - c) into the ellipse equation:\n\nx\u00b2/a\u00b2 + [(1/2)(x - c)]\u00b2 / b\u00b2 = 1\n\nSimplify:\n\nx\u00b2/a\u00b2 + ( (x - c)^2 ) / (4b\u00b2) ) = 1\n\nMultiply both sides by 4a\u00b2b\u00b2 to eliminate denominators:\n\n4b\u00b2x\u00b2 + a\u00b2(x - c)^2 = 4a\u00b2b\u00b2\n\nExpand the (x - c)^2 term:\n\n4b\u00b2x\u00b2 + a\u00b2(x\u00b2 - 2cx + c\u00b2) = 4a\u00b2b\u00b2\n\nCombine like terms:\n\n4b\u00b2x\u00b2 + a\u00b2x\u00b2 - 2a\u00b2c x + a\u00b2c\u00b2 = 4a\u00b2b\u00b2\n\nFactor x\u00b2 terms:\n\n(4b\u00b2 + a\u00b2)x\u00b2 - 2a\u00b2c x + (a\u00b2c\u00b2 - 4a\u00b2b\u00b2) = 0\n\nThis is a quadratic in x. Let me write it as:\n\n[(4b\u00b2 + a\u00b2)]x\u00b2 - [2a\u00b2c]x + [a\u00b2(c\u00b2 - 4b\u00b2)] = 0\n\nLet me denote the quadratic as Ax\u00b2 + Bx + C = 0, where:\n\nA = 4b\u00b2 + a\u00b2\n\nB = -2a\u00b2c\n\nC = a\u00b2(c\u00b2 - 4b\u00b2)\n\nThe solutions x\u2081 and x\u2082 will satisfy:\n\nx\u2081 + x\u2082 = -B/A = (2a\u00b2c)/(4b\u00b2 + a\u00b2)\n\nx\u2081x\u2082 = C/A = [a\u00b2(c\u00b2 - 4b\u00b2)] / (4b\u00b2 + a\u00b2)\n\nSimilarly, since y = (1/2)(x - c), then y\u2081 + y\u2082 = (1/2)(x\u2081 + x\u2082 - 2c) = (1/2)( (2a\u00b2c)/(4b\u00b2 + a\u00b2) - 2c )\n\nLet me compute that:\n\n= (1/2)[ (2a\u00b2c - 2c(4b\u00b2 + a\u00b2)) / (4b\u00b2 + a\u00b2) ]\n\n= (1/2)[ (2a\u00b2c - 8b\u00b2c - 2a\u00b2c) / (4b\u00b2 + a\u00b2) ]\n\n= (1/2)[ (-8b\u00b2c) / (4b\u00b2 + a\u00b2) ]\n\n= (-4b\u00b2c) / (4b\u00b2 + a\u00b2)\n\nBut we also kn