nce perfectly. The pattern can also be interpreted as pronic numbers (products of consecutive integers) with an index shift.\n\n**Final Answer:**  \nThe \\(n\\)-th term of the sequence is \\(\\boxed{n(n - 1)}\\).", "ground_truth_solution": "\nTo determine the general form of the \\(n\\)-th term of the sequence \\(0, 2, 6, 12, 20, 30, 42, 56, 72, 90, \\ldots\\), let's first investigate the differences between consecutive terms.\n\n1. **Calculate the Differences Between Consecutive Terms**:\n   Observe the differences:\n   \\[\n   \\begin{align*}\n   2 - 0 &= 2, \\\\\n   6 - 2 &= 4, \\\\\n   12 - 6 &= 6, \\\\\n   20 - 12 &= 8, \\\\\n   30 - 20 &= 10, \\\\\n   42 - 30 &= 12, \\\\\n   56 - 42 &= 14, \\\\\n   72 - 56 &= 16, \\\\\n   90 - 72 &= 18, \\\\\n   \\text{and so on}.\n   \\end{align*}\n   \\]\n   We see the differences form an arithmetic sequence with the common difference \\(d = 2\\):\n   \\[\n   2, 4, 6, 8, 10, 12, 14, 16, 18, \\ldots\n   \\]\n\n2. **Identify the General Term of the Differences**:\n   The \\(n\\)-th term of the difference sequence can be expressed as:\n   \\[\n   a_n = 2n\n   \\]\n\n3. **Verify the Given Sequence as Product of Consecutive Integers**:\n   The elements of the given sequence can be each written as the product of two consecutive integers:\n   \\[\n   \\begin{align*}\n   0 & = 0 \\times 1, \\\\\n   2 & = 1 \\times 2, \\\\\n   6 & = 2 \\times 3, \\\\\n   12 & = 3 \\times 4, \\\\\n   20 & = 4 \\times 5, \\\\\n   30 & = 5 \\times 6, \\\\\n   42 & = 6 \\times 7, \\\\\n   56 & = 7 \\times 8, \\\\\n   72 & = 8 \\times 9, \\\\\n   90 & = 9 \\times 10.\n   \\end{align*}\n   \\]\n   This suggests the \\(n\\)-th term can be expressed in general as:\n   \\[\n   a_n = (n-1) \\times n\n   \\]\n\n4. **Derive the General Formula**:\n   Using the hypothesis that \\(a_n = (n-1) \\times n\\), we can verify:\n   \\[\n   \\begin{align*}\n   a_{n+1} &= n \\times (n+1), \\\\\n   \\text{and the recursive relationship:} \\\\\n   a_{n+1} - a_n &= n(n+1) - (n-1)n \\\\\n   &= n^2 + n - (n^2 - n) \\\\\n   &= 2n.\n   \\end{align*}\n   \\]\n   We see that the recursive relationship holds true and matches our derived sequence.\n\nTherefore, the general form for the \\(n\\)-th term of this sequence is:\n\\[\na_n = (n-1)n = n(n-1)\n\\]\n\nThus, the \\(n\\)-th term of the sequence is:\n\\[\n\\boxed{a_n = n(n-1)}\n\\]", "domain": "math", "source": "numina_math", "test_cases": null, "starter_code": null}, {"problem": "Let \\( A, B, C, x_{1}, x_{2}, x_{3}, x_{4} \\) be arbitrary real numbers such that \\( x_{1}<x_{2}<x_{3}<x_{4} \\) and let \\( p(x) = A x^{2} + B x + C \\). Prove that\n\n$$\n\\begin{gathered}\n\\qquad \\max _{i=1,2,3,4}\\left|p\\left(x_{i}\\right)-y_{i}\\right| \\geq 1 \\\\\n\\text { where } y_{1}=y_{3}=1, \\quad y_{2}=y_{4}=-1.\n\\end{gathered}\n$$", "deepseek_reasoning": "Okay, so I need to prove that for any quadratic polynomial p(x) = Ax\u00b2 + Bx + C and four real numbers x\u2081 < x\u2082 < x\u2083 < x\u2084, the maximum difference between p(x_i) and y_i is at least 1, where y\u2081 = y\u2083 = 1 and y\u2082 = y\u2084 = -1. Hmm, interesting. Let me think about how to approach this.\n\nFirst, let's restate the problem to make sure I understand it. We have four points on the x-axis: x\u2081, x\u2082, x\u2083, x\u2084, ordered from smallest to largest. The corresponding y-values alternate between 1 and -1: y\u2081 and y\u2083 are 1, y\u2082 and y\u2084 are -1. We need to show that no matter what quadratic polynomial we choose, the maximum vertical distance between the polynomial and these points is at least 1. In other words, for any quadratic, at least one of these four points will have a y-value that's at least 1 unit away from the polynomial's value at that x-coordinate.\n\nHmm. Quadratic polynomials are parabolas, so they can open upwards or downwards. Since we have four points alternating between 1 and -1, maybe there's some sort of oscillation that a quadratic can't match closely? Because quadratics can