q 1\n\\]\nholds where \\( y_1 = y_3 = 1 \\) and \\( y_2 = y_4 = -1 \\), we proceed as follows:\n\n**Proof by Contradiction:**\n\n1. **Assumption:** Suppose there exists a quadratic polynomial \\( p(x) \\) such that \\( |p(x_i) - y_i| < 1 \\) for all \\( i = 1, 2, 3, 4 \\).\n\n2. **Implications for \\( p(x_i) \\):**\n   - At \\( x_1 \\) and \\( x_3 \\): \\( |p(x_i) - 1| < 1 \\) implies \\( 0 < p(x_1) < 2 \\) and \\( 0 < p(x_3) < 2 \\).\n   - At \\( x_2 \\) and \\( x_4 \\): \\( |p(x_i) + 1| < 1 \\) implies \\( -2 < p(x_2) < 0 \\) and \\( -2 < p(x_4) < 0 \\).\n\n3. **Sign Alternation:** The polynomial \\( p(x) \\) alternates in sign across the intervals:\n   - \\( p(x_1) > 0 \\) and \\( p(x_2) < 0 \\) \u21d2 By the Intermediate Value Theorem (IVT), \\( p(x) \\) has a root in \\( (x_1, x_2) \\).\n   - \\( p(x_2) < 0 \\) and \\( p(x_3) > 0 \\) \u21d2 IVT implies a root in \\( (x_2, x_3) \\).\n   - \\( p(x_3) > 0 \\) and \\( p(x_4) < 0 \\) \u21d2 IVT implies a root in \\( (x_3, x_4) \\).\n\n4. **Contradiction:** A quadratic polynomial can have at most two real roots. However, the above argument identifies three distinct intervals each containing a root, which is impossible. This contradiction invalidates the initial assumption.\n\n**Conclusion:** No quadratic polynomial \\( p(x) \\) can satisfy \\( |p(x_i) - y_i| < 1 \\) for all \\( i \\). Therefore, the maximum deviation must satisfy\n\\[\n\\max_{i=1,2,3,4} |p(x_i) - y_i| \\geq 1.\n\\]\n\n\\(\\boxed{\\max _{i=1,2,3,4}\\left|p\\left(x_{i}\\right)-y_{i}\\right| \\geq 1}\\)", "ground_truth_solution": "\nWe need to prove that for any real numbers \\( A, B, C, x_1, x_2, x_3, x_4 \\) where \\( x_1 < x_2 < x_3 < x_4 \\) and \\( p(x) = Ax^2 + Bx + C \\), the following inequality holds:\n\n$$\n\\max_{i=1,2,3,4}\\left|p\\left(x_{i}\\right)-y_{i}\\right| \\geq 1\n$$\nwhere \\( y_1 = y_3 = 1 \\) and \\( y_2 = y_4 = -1 \\).\n\n1. **Assume towards a contradiction** that:\n\n    $$\n    \\left|p\\left(x_{i}\\right)-y_{i}\\right| < 1 \\