 k)^2 = r^2 ...(1)\n\nFor L: (-q/2 - h)^2 + (q/2 - k)^2 = r^2 ...(2)\n\nFor M: ((p + 1 - q)/2 - h)^2 + (q/2 - k)^2 = r^2 ...(3)\n\nSubtract equation (2) from equation (3):\n\n[((p + 1 - q)/2 - h)^2 - (-q/2 - h)^2] + [(q/2 - k)^2 - (q/2 - k)^2] = 0\n\nThe y-terms cancel out. Expand the x-terms:\n\n[( (p +1 - q)/2 - h )^2 - ( (-q/2 - h )^2 )] = 0\n\nLet\u2019s denote A = (p +1 - q)/2 - h and B = -q/2 - h\n\nThen, A^2 - B^2 = (A - B)(A + B) = 0\n\nCompute A - B: [ (p +1 - q)/2 - h ] - [ -q/2 - h ] = (p +1 - q)/2 + q/2 = (p +1)/2\n\nCompute A + B: [ (p +1 - q)/2 - h ] + [ -q/2 - h ] = (p +1 - q - q)/2 - 2h = (p +1 - 2q)/2 - 2h\n\nThus, (A - B)(A + B) = (p +1)/2 * [ (p +1 - 2q)/2 - 2h ] = 0\n\nTherefore, either (p +1)/2 = 0 or [ (p +1 - 2q)/2 - 2h ] = 0\n\nBut (p +1)/2 = 0 implies p = -1. However, point P is on CA, which in our coordinate system is from C(-1,0) to A(1,0). So p \u2208 [-1,1]. If p = -1, then P coincides with C. But in the problem statement, P is on CA, which is a side, so endpoints are allowed. However, if p = -1, then K would be the midpoint of BC, but in general, we need to consider p as variable. So unless p = -1 is enforced, which it isn't, we have the other condition:\n\n(p +1 - 2q)/2 - 2h = 0 => h = (p +1 - 2q)/4\n\nSo h = (p + 1 - 2q)/4.\n\nSimilarly, subtract equation (1) from equation (2):\n\n[(-q/2 - h)^2 + (q/2 - k)^2] - [(p/2 - h)^2 + (1/2 - k)^2] = 0\n\nExpand each term:\n\nFirst term: (-q/2 - h)^2 = (q/2 + h)^2 = q\u00b2/4 + q h + h\u00b2\n\nSecond term: (q/2 - k)^2 = q\u00b2/4 - q k + k\u00b2\n\nThird term: (p/2 - h)^2 = p\u00b2/4 - p h + h\u00b2\n\nFourth term: (1/2 - k)^2 = 1/4 - k + k\u00b2\n\nThus, substituting:\n\n[ q\u00b2/4 + q h + h\u00b2 + q\u00b2/4 - q k + k\u00b2 ] - [ p\u00b2/4 - p h + h\u00b2 + 1/4 - k + k\u00b2 ] = 0\n\nSimplify:\n\nFirst bracket: q\u00b2/4 + q h + h\u00b2 + q\u00b2/4 - q k + k\u00b2 = q\u00b2/2 + q h + h\u00b2 - q k + k\u00b2\n\nSecond bracket: p\u00b2/4 - p h + h\u00b2 + 1/4 - k + k\u00b2 = p\u00b2/4 - p h + h\u00b2 + 1/4 - k + k\u00b2\n\nSubtract second bracket from first bracket:\n\n[ q\u00b2/2 + q h + h\u00b2 - q k + k\u00b2 ] - [ p\u00b2/4 - p h + h\u00b2 + 1/4 - k + k\u00b2 ] = \n\nq\u00b2/2 + q h - q k - p\u00b2/4 + p h - 1/4 + k = 0\n\nSimplify:\n\nGroup terms:\n\nh(q + p) + k(-q +1) + q\u00b2/2 - p\u00b2/4 -1/4 = 0\n\nNow, we already have h = (p +1 - 2q)/4 from earlier. Let's substitute h into this equation.\n\nSo h = (p +1 - 2q)/4. Then:\n\nh(q + p) = [(p +1 - 2q)/4](q + p)\n\nk(-q +1) = k(1 - q)\n\nSo the equation becomes:\n\n[(p +1 - 2q)/4](q + p) + k(1 - q) + q\u00b2/2 - p\u00b2/4 -1/4 = 0\n\nLet me compute each part step by step.\n\nFirst term: [(p +1 - 2q)(q + p)] / 4\n\nExpand (p +1 - 2q)(q + p):\n\n= p(q + p) +1*(q + p) -2q(q + p)\n\n= pq + p\u00b2 + q + p - 2q\u00b2 - 2pq\n\n= (pq - 2pq) + p\u00b2 + q + p - 2q\u00b2\n\n= -pq + p\u00b2 + q + p - 2q\u00b2\n\nThus, first term: (-pq + p\u00b2 + q + p - 2q\u00b2)/4\n\nSecond term: k(1 - q)\n\nThird term: q\u00b2/2\n\nFourth term: -p\u00b2/4\n\nFifth term: -1/4\n\nPutting all together:\n\n[ (-pq + p\u00b2 + q + p - 2q\u00b2)/4 ] + k(1 - q) + q\u00b2/2 - p\u00b2/4 -1/4 = 0\n\nCombine like terms:\n\np\u00b2/4 - p\u00b2/4 cancels.\n\n-pq/4 remains.\n\nq/4 remains.\n\np/4 remains.\n\n-2q\u00b2/4 = -q\u00b2/2.\n\nThen + q\u00b2/2 cancels with -q\u00b2/2.\n\nThen we have:\n\n-pq/4 + q/4 + p/4 + k(1 - q) -1/4 = 0\n\nSo:\n\n(-pq + q + p)/4 + k(1 - q) -1/4 = 0\n\nMultiply all terms by 4:\n\n(-pq + q + p) + 4k(1 - q) -1 = 0\n\nSimplify:\n\n-pq + q + p -1 + 4k(1 - q) = 0\n\nTherefore:\n\n4k(1 - q) = pq - q - p +1\n\nFactor the right-hand side:\n\npq - q - p +1 = q(p -1) - (p -1) = (q -1)(p -1)\n\nThus:\n\n4k(1 - q) = (q -1)(p -1) = -(1 - q)(p -1)\n\nTherefore:\n\n4k(1 - q) = - (1 - q)(p -1)\n\nAssuming 1 - q \u2260 0 (i.e., q \u22601), we can divide both sides by (1 - q):\n\n4k = -(p -1) => k = -(p -1)/4 = (1 - p)/4\n\nIf q =1, then Q coincides with B(0,1). But Q is on AB, which in our coordinate system goes from A(1,0