c{u}{(v + 1)^2} \\\\\n     \\frac{1}{v + 1} & -\\frac{u}{(v + 1)^2}\n     \\end{matrix} \\right| = \\frac{u}{(v + 1)^2}\n     \\]\n\n4. **Joint PDF Transformation**:\n   - The joint pdf of \\(\\xi\\) and \\(\\eta\\) is \\(f_{\\xi, \\eta}(x, y) = \\lambda^2 e^{-\\lambda (x + y)}\\).\n   - Substitute the inverse transformations into the joint pdf and multiply by the Jacobian determinant:\n     \\[\n     f_{U, V}(u, v) = \\lambda^2 e^{-\\lambda u} \\cdot \\frac{u}{(v + 1)^2}\n     \\]\n\n5. **Factorization of Joint PDF**:\n   - The joint pdf factors into:\n     \\[\n     f_{U, V}(u, v) = \\left( \\lambda^2 u e^{-\\lambda u} \\right) \\cdot \\left( \\frac{1}{(v + 1)^2} \\right)\n     \\]\n   - Recognize that \\(\\lambda^2 u e^{-\\lambda u}\\) is the pdf of a Gamma distribution (sum of two iid exponentials) and \\(\\frac{1}{(v + 1)^2}\\) is the pdf of the ratio \\(\\xi / \\eta\\).\n\n6. **Independence Conclusion**:\n   - Since the joint pdf factors into the product of the marginal pdfs of \\(U\\) and \\(V\\), we conclude that \\(U\\) and \\(V\\) are independent.\n\nThus, the random variables \\(\\xi + \\eta\\) and \\(\\xi / \\eta\\) are independent when \\(\\xi\\) and \\(\\eta\\) are independent and identically distributed exponential variables.\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\xi + \\eta \\text{ and } \\xi/\\eta \\text{ are independent}}\n\\]", "ground_truth_solution": "\nLet $\\xi$ and $\\eta$ be two independent, identically distributed (i.i.d.) random variables with exponential distributions. Specifically, we assume that $\\xi$ and $\\eta$ have the following probability density functions (PDF):\n\n\\[\nf_{\\xi}(x) = \\lambda e^{-\\lambda x}, \\quad x \\geq 0,\n\\]\n\\[\nf_{\\eta}(y) = \\lambda e^{-\\lambda y}, \\quad y \\geq 0.\n\\]\n\nOur goal is to show that the random variables $\\xi + \\eta$ and $\\frac{\\xi}{\\eta}$ are independent.\n\n#### Step 1: Transformation to New Variables\n\nConsider the transformation from $(\\xi, \\eta)$ to new variables $(\\zeta, \\theta)$ defined by